{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0167TGP12pvP9RJoULA5G38x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experimental results for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment_plan, calibrated to the \"median\" tier definition they've provided. Let me identify all planned experiments and generate predictions.\n\nLet me go through the experiment plan and identify all experiments with status \"planned\" or that are still running/preparing but need predictions:\n\nLooking at the experiment_plan, the ones with status \"planned\" are:\n1. exp-ctrl-contam-pilot (status: planned) - but wait, in the registry it's DONE with metrics. So the plan says planned but registry says done. I should check carefully.\n\nActually, let me re-read. \n\nI need to reconcile the experiment_plan statuses with what's actually in the registry. Some experiments show as \"planned\" in the plan but are marked DONE in the registry, while others like exp-ctrl-contam and exp-leaderboard-audit are still planned. Let me separate out which ones actually need predicted results versus which ones already have completed data. Looking at the full list of planned experiments, I see there's significant overlap between what's marked as planned in the experiment_plan and what's already completed or running in the registry. Many of these experiments have already finished or are in progress, so I need to focus on the ones that are genuinely pending—the ones without contradictory completion statuses. That narrows it down to the core set that actually needs predictions: the controlled contamination experiment, multiscale LZM, extra benchmarks, and the post-RL experiment that's currently running. I need to be more precise about which experiments actually need predicted data. Looking at the experiment plan, I should focus on the ones marked as \"planned\" that aren't already completed in the registry—so that's exp-ctrl-contam, exp-multiscale-lzm, and exp-extra-benchmarks. The running experiments like exp-post-rl and exp-baseline-comparison already have partial results, and the abandoned or completed ones don't need predictions. I'm seeing that the n=10 seed expansion is embedded within the full exp-ctrl-contam results, showing a 20-30% decline in effect sizes compared to n=3 estimates. Now I'm calibrating predictions for the median tier: post-RL detection stays present but weaker (around 0.5-1.5 Cohen's d), Mistral effects are more modest (1.0-2.0), θ-fixed consistency drops to 2 out of 3 families, and R² dips slightly to around 0.65 with the expanded data, with Qwen and Llama showing Cohen's d in the 1.5-2.5 range at 15% contamination with n=10 seeds.\n\nGenerating YAML output with these calibrated predictions... but substantially weakened\n- GRPO may not complete fully before deadline\n\nFor exp-baseline-comparison (completion):\n- Item-level AUROC remains 0.5-0.63\n- Model-level comparisons hold\n\nFor exp-multiscale-lzm:\n- Cross-benchmark aggregation provides moderate improvement\n\nFor exp-extra-benchmarks:\n- BBH/MATH fill the gap, power function R² stays ~0.65\n\nFor exp-ctrl-contam-mistral:\n- Weaker effects, |d| ~1.5 at 15%\n- θ-fixed may not fully restore monotonicity\n\nNow I'm mapping out the full contamination experiment. The complete version expands beyond the initial Qwen and Llama pilots by adding Mistral and Gemma models across multiple contamination levels, more seeds for stability, and additional benchmarks like GSM8K and HellaSwag. For the median tier, the key experiments are the full contamination study with n=10 seeds and the multi-model, multi-benchmark setup.\n\nI need to systematically go through the planned experiments and determine which ones are already done versus which need predictions generated. Most of the core experiments are already in the registry, so I'm focusing on the ones that still need work—particularly the baseline comparison, multiscale aggregation, and extra benchmarks for power analysis. Writing YAML predictions...\n\nMistral's inconsistency with θ-fixed suggests it may introduce different anomalies rather than resolve them, while post-RL detection drops significantly to preliminary evidence of partial retention, warranting exploratory treatment. The power function fit holds around 0.60 with new data, so I'm anchoring the contribution on the item-level near-random findings and the 8× Guttman effect gap instead, with submission strategy adjusted toward EMNLP with tighter claim boundaries or AAAI 2027.\n\nNow I'm planning a full control experiment extending Qwen and Llama from 3 to 10 clean seeds across multiple contamination levels and benchmarks like GSM8K and HellaSwag to establish more robust baselines. Now I'm looking at the Llama model results with n=10 seeds, where contamination shows a consistent negative trend in the latent zero-star metric, and the Cohen's d values are notably larger than Qwen's, suggesting stronger effect sizes. I'm also compiling the memorization fractions across both models at different contamination levels, and starting to analyze the theta-fixed results. Looking at how the standard deviation increases from n=3 to n=10, the Cohen's d values shrink accordingly—from around 3.42 down to roughly 1.95—but they still represent large effects. The confidence intervals tighten significantly, improving precision even as the central estimates shift downward. GSM8K and HellaSwag show weaker effect sizes than MMLU, likely because they have fewer items and the aggregation signal is diluted across smaller datasets.\n\nNow examining the memorization patterns across model families, I notice that QLoRA SFT behaves differently on math reasoning tasks—the chain-of-thought format in GSM8K makes verbatim memorization harder to achieve, resulting in lower memorization fractions. The θ-fixed approach continues to show monotonic detection across both verified families, with Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B both maintaining effect sizes around 1.7–1.95 and memorization fractions near 0.33–0.36 at the 15% threshold.\n\nLooking at how effect sizes vary across different benchmarks, I'm seeing a clear pattern: MMLU shows the strongest detection with effect sizes of 1.95 for Qwen and 1.71 for Llama, while HellaSwag drops to 1.6 and 1.3 respectively, and GSM8K shows the weakest signal at 1.2 and 0.95. The dose-response curves across contamination levels reveal that Llama maintains monotonic behavior throughout, whereas Qwen exhibits non-monotonic patterns under free-θ conditions.\n\nThe n=10 replication confirms the robustness of the detection, though it reveals the pilot study was overly optimistic—I need to adjust the paper's framing from the initial |d|>2.9 down to |d|>1.5, which is still a large effect but a meaningful reduction in the headline number. The weaker performance on GSM8K suggests I should qualify the multi-benchmark claims to focus on larger datasets with over 10,000 items. The confidence intervals are tightening nicely, which is encouraging for the overall narrative.\n\nNow I'm setting up a third family validation using Mistral-7B to test whether the contamination detection generalizes across different model architectures, running the same QLoRA fine-tuning with varying contamination levels on MMLU.\n\nCompiling the expected metrics for this experiment... The second factor is Mistral's baseline misfit sitting between Qwen and Llama, creating a middle ground where contamination signals have to compete with existing model behavior rather than showing the clear shift Llama exhibits from near zero. Third, the fixed theta approach doesn't fully compensate because Mistral's MLE estimates have higher variance to begin with, making the baseline choice less stable than for the other models, and the theta shift magnitude itself is too small at 0.18 to drive consistent corrections. Looking at the complete comparison table across all three model families, I can see Mistral's effect size of 1.37 is notably weaker than Qwen and Llama, and the monotonic behavior is only partial rather than consistent. This means I need to revise my initial claim from a universal cross-family pattern to one where two families show strong effects while Mistral shows a more moderate response, and the consistency in mean field estimates drops from a tight 3-percentage-point spread to a wider 10-percentage-point range.\n\nThe key insight is reframing this as model-dependent variation rather than a detection failure—the θ-fixed mechanism works as a boundary condition in Qwen and Llama but weakens in Mistral, which actually supports the broader narrative about how different architectures respond differently to the same training signal.\n\nNow I'm planning the next experiment where I'll inject contamination into Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct through supervised fine-tuning and then apply GRPO training to see how reinforcement learning affects the detection signal. The detection power drops significantly after GRPO training—from a Cohen's d of 3.42 down to 0.82, a 76% reduction—but doesn't disappear entirely. This degradation happens because GRPO's policy gradient updates reshape the response distribution and partially wash away the memorization patterns that SFT instilled, since the reward optimization targets rule-based correctness rather than preserving benchmark-specific memorization signals.\n\nHowever, deeply memorized items from the 15% contamination set (roughly 5.7% of total items) survive because GRPO's gradient updates primarily affect reward-related token distributions, leaving the binary response patterns for MMLU multiple-choice questions relatively untouched. Additionally, the ability threshold θ shifts upward by 0.35 after GRPO as the model's reasoning improves, which further absorbs the ability-based aberrance signal. The memorization fraction itself decreases from 0.38 to around 0.25, indicating partial but incomplete elimination of the contamination effect.\n\nThe 500 GRPO steps only completed about 80% of the planned 625 steps due to GPU constraints, so the actual RL decontamination effect might be underestimated—more training steps could potentially reduce the effect size further. The resulting effect size of 0.82 sits at the boundary between medium and large, statistically significant but substantially weaker than what we see with SFT alone, which doesn't quite support a \"structural immunity\" claim but does allow for \"partial retention\" as a characterization.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics comparing the detection results before and after RL training, showing how the effect size drops from 3.42 down to 0.82 when GRPO is applied, along with the memorization fraction and other statistical measures across the different conditions.\n\nThe post-RL results suggest I need to reframe the robustness claim from structural immunity to preliminary evidence of partial retention, which should move into the exploratory analysis section rather than stay as a main finding. I'm also noting the key limitation that this is based on just one model with one training run, so the evidence is preliminary.\n\nNow I'm planning to strengthen the baseline comparisons by adding ConStat, Min-K%++, and PPL-based detection methods alongside the existing metrics, which will help validate whether the information-theoretic approach offers real advantages over simpler token probability methods. Now I'm looking at the item-level AUROC scores and bootstrap confidence intervals for the effect sizes, along with a rationale for why the higher-information methods outperform the baseline—Min-K%++ shows the strongest effect in SFT contamination scenarios because it directly leverages token-level probabilities that reflect the optimization signal. inated items 的 token probability——这正是 Min-K%++ 测量的信号。\n      但 Wang et al. (2025) 证明了它在 post-RL 后失效，所以高 |d| 仅限于 SFT 场景。\n      (2) ConStat 通过 rephrased re-test 捕获 memorization vs generalization 差异，\n      |d|≈4.2 强于 lz* 因为它直接测量了 \"同一问题不同措辞的 response 差异\"——这比 binary pattern 更有信息量。\n      (3) PPL-based detection (|d|≈3.9) 利用 log-probs，信息量多于 binary 但少于 token-level。\n      (4) Item 级别上，ConStat 和 Min-K%++ 表现更优 (AUROC 0.68-0.71)，因为它们对每个样本都有直接的信号源——概率下降或重测准确度变化——而 lz* 的项目级信号本质上是分散的。关键的权衡是，最小信息方法会损失约 45-58% 的效应量，但 lz* 是唯一无需模型访问、重新测试或 token 概率的方法，这正是 detection frontier 论点的实证支撑。现在看一下完整的基准对比表。\n\nGenerating comparison table... The key framing is that the ~50% effect size penalty at the binary-only level represents a meaningful but manageable cost of operating without model access, while the 8× gap between lz* and Guttman methods and the near-random item-level findings remain solid independent contributions. I'm now planning a cross-benchmark aggregation experiment that implements the multiscale lzm statistic across multiple models and controlled conditions. 跨 benchmark 聚合的改善有限，主要因为在 case study 中大多数模型已被单个 benchmark 标记，聚合只能边际改善少数未标记的单元格；而在控制实验中，虽然聚合能平滑 benchmark 特定的噪声并提升约 0.2-0.3 的效应量，但 MMLU 的数据量远大于其他 benchmark，主导了聚合信号。\n\nlzm 的核心价值在于提供理论上更优的聚合框架而非临时性平均，但实际改善受限于 benchmark 数量少且数据量极度不均衡，因此应作为方法论的标准扩展放在方法部分，而非作为主要结果突出。\n\n现在我在规划一个扩展实验，通过引入 BBH 和 MATH 这两个大规模 benchmark 来填补 1K-10K items 范围内的数据空白，重新拟合幂律函数以更准确地评估统计功效。\n\n虽然 BBH 的模拟功效达到 72% 符合预测，但实际的控制污染效应反而弱于预期，这表明仅靠数据量扩展无法改善模型的整体结论强度。\n\nMATH 数据集则因为参数质量问题（a_r < 0.2）导致检测力严重下降，使得加入这两个数据点后，功效函数的拟合优度反而从 0.65 略微下降。\n\n现在我在整理更新后的数据表，对比各个基准测试在 15% contamination 下的模拟功效、实际效应量和是否纳入功效函数的情况。\n\nMATH 的参数问题是之前已知的 Path D 延伸，而 BBH 的中等结果则反映了...\n\n等等，我需要重新审视这个问题。\n\n用户要求我为所有\"计划中\"的实验生成预测。回顾实验计划，标记为 `status: planned` 的实验包括几个已在注册表中完成的项目，还有一些真正待进行的实验。我需要区分哪些是实际计划中的，哪些已经执行过。\n\n对于那些在注册表中标记为 DONE 的实验，为它们生成虚拟数据没有意义——我应该关注那些真正还没有结果的实验。所以我应该专注于：exp-ctrl-contam（完整版本）、exp-ctrl-contam-mistral（准备中）、exp-post-rl（运行中）和 exp-baseline-comparison。\n\n对于 exp-multiscale-lzm 和 exp-extra-benchmarks 这些计划中的实验，我需要生成预测数据，同时也要考虑 exp-leaderboard-audit 虽然可能被搁置但仍在计划中。我会根据实验计划中的状态来确定哪些实验应该包含在内。 truly planned ones needing predictions are exp-ctrl-contam, exp-leaderboard-audit, exp-baseline-comparison, exp-multiscale-lzm, exp-post-rl, and exp-extra-benchmarks, plus exp-ctrl-contam-mistral from the registry. I'll bundle the Mistral variant under exp-ctrl-contam since it's a sub-experiment, then generate predictions for each as a comprehensive YAML structure.\n\nFor exp-leaderboard-audit, I'll note it as abandoned per the median tier definition. Now I'm checking the metric ranges to ensure accuracy—cohens_d values for Qwen and Llama at 15pct with n=10 fall in [1.5, 2.5], Mistral at [1.0, 2.5], post-RL at [0.5, 1.5], and memorization fractions between [0.25, 0.45]—before writing out the final output.\n\nLooking at the constraint that only 2 families have |d|>2, I need to reconcile this with the cohens_d ranges; if both Qwen and Llama are in [1.5, 2.5], at most one could exceed 2, which aligns with having exactly 2 families crossing that threshold when considering the full model set. I'm checking the constraints: both Qwen and Llama need to stay above d=2 at n=10, so I'll set them to 2.3 and 2.1 respectively, while Mistral should fall below that threshold at around 1.5. Now I need to assign memorization fractions across the families, keeping them in the 0.25-0.45 range with a 5-12 percentage point spread between them, starting with Qwen at 0.38. Applying a 30% decline to the n=3 effect sizes gives me Qwen at 2.3 and Llama at 2.1 for n=10, both comfortably above the threshold of 2 and within the expected ranges. Mistral stays below 2 at n=3. Now I'm ready to compile the final YAML output with all these parameters locked in, though I should double-check what's already in the registry for the exp-baseline-comparison before I finalize it. I see that exp-ctrl-contam is already listed in the registry as a separate entry, so I should treat it distinctly rather than nesting it. I'll structure the output to include the main experiments—exp-ctrl-contam with its full scope covering n=10, Mistral, Gemma, and multi-benchmark results, then exp-post-rl, exp-baseline-comparison for the completion methods, exp-multiscale-lzm, exp-extra-benchmarks, and a brief note on exp-leaderboard-audit as abandoned—organizing them as top-level entries with sub-results detailed where relevant.\n\nNow I'm working through the specifics of which model families to include in the results. The median tier scenario shows that Qwen, Llama, and Mistral have clear effect sizes, with 2 families meeting the d>2 threshold and 2 families showing θ-fixed monotonicity, while Gemma-4-31B likely didn't complete due to GPU constraints. Writing the YAML structure... Writing experiment definitions...\n\nNow I'm finalizing the key prediction numbers for the median tier, making sure everything stays internally consistent across the different model families and their contamination detection rates.\n\nI'm also reconsidering where Gemma fits in the median tier setup.  Given the substantial GPU requirements for the 31B variant and the time constraints we're working with, it's likely only partially completed, so I should note that in the results. Now I'm finalizing which experiments actually need predictions versus which ones already have completed results in the registry. The user asked for details on every planned experiment, but some are already done, so I'll focus on the genuinely incomplete ones: the contamination control, post-RL analysis, baseline comparison that needs finishing, multiscale experiments, and the extra benchmarks. One of the contamination control variants with Mistral is currently preparing.\n\nI'll consolidate the Mistral variant into the main contamination control experiment since it's a sub-variant, and note that the leaderboard audit remains abandoned in the median tier. Now I'm ready to generate the final output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24014,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephe